==========================================
NETWORKING DAY 14 — PROJECT 1
Network Recon Script (netrecon.sh)
==========================================

--- RUNNING THE SCRIPT ---
chmod +x netrecon.sh                  # make it executable
bash -n netrecon.sh                    # syntax check only, runs nothing
./netrecon.sh --help                    # usage
./netrecon.sh                            # auto-detect the subnet of the default-route interface
./netrecon.sh 192.168.80.0/24             # scan a subnet you choose (must be YOUR lab network)
./netrecon.sh 127.0.0.1/32                 # safe dry run on your own machine
NETRECON_DIR=~/my-reports ./netrecon.sh     # save reports somewhere else

--- WHAT THE SCRIPT DOES ---
1. detect_target / validate_target / confirm_target
      private ranges only, /24 or smaller, retype the target to continue
2. sweep        sudo nmap -sn <target> -oA sweep            ping sweep, who is alive
3. scan_hosts   sudo nmap -F -sV --version-light -T4 --host-timeout 90s -iL live_hosts.txt -oA ports
4. summarize    awk joins hostnames + MACs + open ports into one table, sorted with sort -V

--- FILES ONE RUN CREATES (in ~/netrecon-reports/<date_time>/) ---
sweep.nmap  sweep.gnmap  sweep.xml        ping sweep, three formats
ports.nmap  ports.gnmap  ports.xml         port scan, three formats
live_hosts.txt                              IPs that answered the sweep
summary.txt                                  the final table

--- NMAP FLAGS USED (and where you met them) ---
-sn                      host discovery only, no port scan          Day 04
-F                       top 100 ports                               Day 04
-sV --version-light       service names/versions, light probing       Day 06
-T4                       faster timing                               Day 04-05
--host-timeout 90s         give up on one slow host after 90 s         Day 04
-iL <file>                 read targets from a file
-oA <name>                  save .nmap + .gnmap + .xml at once          Day 07

--- READING THE THREE OUTPUT FORMATS ---
.nmap      normal text, same as the terminal; has the "MAC Address:" lines
.gnmap     grepable, one line per host:  Host: <ip> (<name>)<TAB>Ports: 22/open/tcp//ssh///, ...
.xml       structured, meant for other programs to parse

--- TEXT-PROCESSING RECIPES USED (Linux journal Days 15-16) ---
awk '/Status: Up/ {print $2}' sweep.gnmap          # pull the IPs of live hosts
awk -F'\t' '...'                                     # split a line on TAB instead of spaces
sort -V                                               # sort IP addresses in natural order (.2 before .136)
mktemp -d                                              # make a throwaway working directory

--- COMPARING TWO RUNS (idea for Part E2) ---
ls -dt ~/netrecon-reports/*/ | head -2               # newest two report folders
sort live_hosts.txt > /tmp/now.txt                      # comm needs sorted input
comm -13 /tmp/before.txt /tmp/now.txt                    # lines only in the NEW run  (new hosts)
comm -23 /tmp/before.txt /tmp/now.txt                    # lines only in the OLD run  (missing hosts)

--- CHECKING THE RESULT AGAINST REALITY ---
ss -tuln                      # what is really listening on this machine (Linux journal Day 05)
ip route                       # confirm which subnet you are on BEFORE scanning

--- RULES TO KEEP ---
Only scan networks you own or have written permission for.
Read a script before you run it.
Confirm the subnet with ip route before every run.

